  "body": "Given the following code:\r\n```html\r\n<div id=\"magic\">foo</div>\r\n<div id=\"magic\">bar</div>\r\n\r\n<script>\r\n  console.log(magic)\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn Chrome and Safari, the return is an `HTMLCollection`.\r\nOn Firefox, the return is the `Element` of the first `div`.\r\n\r\nIn the section [`7.3.3 Named access on the Window object`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#named-access-on-the-window-object) it's a little unclear about what should happen in this case.\r\nIt only says that:\r\n> - `HTML elements` that have an `id` content attribute whose value is _name_ and...\r\n\r\nSince should have only one element with the given `id` ([spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-id-attribute)), it looks like that Firefox is assuming that there is only one element with the given `id`, while Chrome and Safari are fetching for every element with the given `id`.\r\n\r\nIs it a de-facto implementation-defined behaviour? Or am I missing something?",
